Fillets: a walk ends at the analytic piece after its guide only where its
other contact lags a little too, not where a ball that stays put keeps one
contact where that piece starts while its other contact still turns round
the tube it touches (mitcad#1427).

A wall's edge with a top is rounded with R up to a convex corner of the
wall with a cylinder, whose rim with the top an earlier rounding of R (a
torus) rounds. The chain goes from the wall's edge with the top (an
analytic piece, a cylinder) over the wall's edge with the torus (walked)
to the wall's edge with the cylinder (an analytic piece). Over the
torus's edge the ball first rolls on the top and the wall up to where its
contact on the top reaches the torus's edge with the top; there its
centre lies on the torus's centre circle, and from there on the ball
stays put: its contact on the wall stays at one point, the start of the
next analytic piece, while its other contact turns round the torus's
section from the top down to the cylinder (a quarter of the section).

ChFi3d_ReachedAnalyticPiece (1301) ended the walk where either contact lay
within ten times the spine's tolerance of the point where the analytic
piece after the guide starts (going back: where the one before it ends),
for a walk whose other contact lags a few micrometres across a slight
kink of the spine. At the ball that stays put the wall's contact is there
already, so the walk ended with the piece on the top and the wall: the
part of the ball between was missing, and the build of the result failed
on the gap ("Knots interval values too close", "U1 == U2") or, on a
synthetic block, made an invalid body. Walked the other way, from the
cylinder's piece, the ball's part was there.

- ChFi3d_ReachedAnalyticPiece: the other contact must also lie within a
  tenth of the analytic piece's section (the distance between its two
  points there) of that piece's point on its side.

On a D-shaped block (a cylinder of 5 cut by a wall, its top rim rounded
with 1) whose wall's edges with the top, the torus and the cylinder are
rounded with 1: valid, each top corner one face on the ball's sphere
within 1e-5; before, the rounding threw, or (radius 2 on a cylinder of 8)
the result was invalid.

